  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 230 AM EST Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Above normal temperatures and tranquil weather \nthrough Saturday.\n\nHigh pressure remain in control into Saturday, with dry conditions \nand above average temperatures expected. High clouds will be on the \nincrease today and especially tonight into Saturday as an upper \nlevel trough approaches from the west and low pressure begins to \ndevelop off the Southeast coast. High temperatures today will range \nfrom the mid 60s to around 70 and overnight lows will generally drop \nback into the 30s inland and 40s closer to the coast. Similar \ntemperatures are anticipated for Saturday with highs generally in \nthe mid 60s to around 70. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Coastal low pressure brings increasing rain chances \nSunday into Sunday night, especially across eastern portions of the \narea. Confidence remains low regarding the western extent of \nprecipitation\n\nAttention remains focused on coastal low pressure developing off the \nSoutheast coast this weekend before tracking northeastward offshore \nSunday into Sunday night. While confidence continues to increase in \nthe overall synoptic evolution, considerable uncertainity remains \nregarding to the NW extent of the associated precipitation \nshield/QPF. \n\nThe latest 00z GFS and ECMWF are now favoring a more offshore \nsolution, with more limited precipitation inland (especially the \nECWMF). Additionally, the 00z EPS has trended much drier compared to \nprevious cycles, lending support to a less expansive precipitation \nshield. However, the Canadian, RRFS, and AI EPS continue to depict a \nmuch wetter solution with precipitation extending farther inland. \nDiving into the 00z EPS, probabilities of 1\" or greater of QPF were \nonly ~10 to 20% across central VA/Richmond and ~20 to 40% near \nNorfolk and closer to the coast. These are drastically lower from \nthe 18z and especially the 12z run which had probs of ~40-60% for \n1\"+ across much of the forecast area. The trends in the GFS, ECMWF, \nand associated ensembles support a somewhat more conservative QPF \nforecast, especially along and west of I-95, while maintaining the \npotential for more appreciable rainfall across eastern portions of \nthe area. \n\nImpacts wise, rain chances increase late Saturday into Sunday, with \nthe highest PoPs favored over the eastern half of the area at this \ntime. Rain chances diminish Sunday evening into Sunday night as the \nlow rapidly moves NE away from the coast. In addition to the rain, \nthere could still also be a brief period of breezy NE to SE winds \nalong the coast on Sunday, but the system will be moving fast enough \nto limit any significant impacts. Finally, given that the winds will \nlikely be shifting and we will be coming out of the lower end of the \nastronomical high tide cycle, only minor tidal flooding is expected \nin the worst case scenario.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Drier conditions return early next week before \nanother storm system potentially impacts the area by Thanksgiving. \n\nLow pressure continues northeastward away from the Mid-Atlantic \ncoast Monday. Any linger precipitation, primarily over NE portions \nof the area, should gradually come to an end by Monday afternoon as \nthe system departs. High pressure then builds into the region, with \ngenerally dry conditions favored into the middle of next week. After \nthis, there is an increasing signal of some type of low pressure \ndevelopment by Wednesday or Thanksgiving along the frontal boundary \nsouth of the area. Unfortunately the 00z guidance was not much help \nwith the GFS, ECMWF, and Canadian all showing differing solutions.\nThis is still roughly a week out and there will be plenty of time to \npin down any details. However, given the +2 sigma high to the north, \nany low pressure to our south will lead to increased NE winds and \nthe potential for some tidal flooding as we will be going into \nanother King Tide cycle.",
